    {
      "id": "2074576029499240813",
      "text": "\"Loop engineering\" isn't the next step after \"prompt engineering.\" It's the bill. \n\nPrompt engineering was always technical debt. Loop engineering just made it visible.\n\nIn a post published in June, Peter Steinberger said out loud what many had been quietly thinking: you shouldn't be prompting coding agents anymore — you should be designing loops that prompt your agents. Boris Cherny of Anthropic described a similar shift: he no longer prompts Claude directly. He builds loops that prompt Claude for him. Addy Osmani mapped the progression clearly: prompt is one layer, context is another, then the harness (the tools, decision logic, error handling, and everything else that turns a model into something that can actually complete work). Sitting on top of all of it is the loop. The industry is celebrating this as a breakthrough. And it really is — for loops. But for everything built below them, it's the end of the road.\n\nHere's the part nobody wants to say out loud. If loops are now what prompt the agents, then every prompt library someone spent the last two or three years carefully curating is no longer an asset. It's scaffolding that has been removed. The individual prompts still work — that was never the issue. The problem is that the step they were standing on has been cut away from above. What used to be the top layer of the stack is now just one component inside something larger.\n\nThis isn't just about one person's carefully tuned prompts suddenly breaking. It's about the entire abstraction layer moving up. Once a harness sits above your prompts, those prompts stop being things you own and curate. They become implementation details the harness generates on the fly.\n\nWe've seen this pattern before. In the early 2000s, SEO experts who had spent years mastering meta tags and keyword density woke up to PageRank and realized their hard-won expertise wasn't wrong — it was simply operating at the wrong layer of the stack. Google didn't invalidate their skill. It just moved the game upstairs. The same thing is happening now with prompt engineering.\n\nThe people building loops today are doing to prompt engineering what Google did to meta-tag SEO. They're mostly not saying it out loud yet — loop engineering is still a new term, and nobody wants to look like they're dismissing an entire community that's still processing the shift. But the economics are straightforward. Generating prompts has become nearly free. Prompt libraries have gone from valuable assets to something close to worthless. What remains scarce is judgment: knowing which loop to build in the first place, when to let it run, and when to step in or shut it down. That was always the real skill. Everything else was just the layer we happened to be working in at the time.\n\nIf you built a prompt library over the last few years and you're feeling defensive reading this, that's a useful signal. The move isn't to defend the library. It's to notice which layer of the stack you were actually working on, and move up to the next one. Building loops isn't harder than writing prompts. It just requires admitting that the old layer is no longer where the leverage is.\n\nWhat layer of the stack were you actually working on this year?\n\nSave this. You'll want it back when the next \"prompt engineering course\" launches and you're trying to remember exactly why you scrolled past it.\n\nFollow for the next one.",
      "author": {
        "id": "2071873252784316416",
        "name": "EchoMind",
        "screenName": "ai0echomind",
        "profileImageUrl": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2071909404392194048/jw3vpyBr_normal.jpg",
